    "changelog": [
      "Core Principles: Emphasised that ranking should demote candidates where remaining words can change agent, polarity, head noun, pronoun antecedent, modifier attachment, or verb sense — to focus fixes on placement errors rather than candidacy.",
      "When to Segment: Made candidacy rules more explicit about safe surface signals and added a short, explicit list of common disambiguating follow-ups (relative/complementizers, negation, postverbal obliques, short temporal/prepositional fragments, range markers, vague pronouns) that should be treated as likely to overturn prior text and therefore ranked lower.",
      "Priority Rules: Tightened the positive criterion for top-ranked cuts: prefer cuts only when the preceding stretch contains a stable predication with its core participants or a self-contained adjunct; added concrete demotion signals (e.g., a comma followed by a relative pronoun, or an immediate following token from the disambiguating list) so risky boundaries are ranked lower rather than removed.",
      "Decision Procedure: Clarified the two-step flow: mark generously, then place risky candidates low; explicitly instruct demotion (not forbidding) for boundaries that end immediately before the listed disambiguating signals and to supply conservative, low-confidence candidates at safe punctuation/conjunctions only when needed to meet the minimum-tag requirement."
    ],
    "next_changelog": [
      "Updated [Role] to emphasise generous candidacy while delegating risk management to ranking and to remind not to rewrite the source text (clarifies behaviour without adding new forbidden positions).",
      "Rewrote [Never Segment] to remove repetition of the frozen output-format constraints (the punctuation/start/end/spacing/minimum rules are now only in [Output Rules]) and to tighten the definition of truly forbidden splits (only positions impossible at any budget).",
      "Replaced and extended examples in both example sections to show frequent placement-failure patterns (polarity before negation, verb sense before postverbal obliques, modifier-head attachments, pronoun antecedent resolution, short temporal/prepositional fragments) with correct segmentation choices so the model learns safe placements by example rather than extra hard rules.",
      "Removed example redundancy and kept the total number of examples within limits while making the examples demonstrate the critic's dominant error modes."
    ]
  },
