    "changelog": [
      "When to Segment: Added a concise list of disambiguating follow-ups (main verb/voice, noun heads, postnominal locatives, negation/polarity markers, post‑verbal obliques, pronoun antecedents, short temporal/prepositional complements, multiword adjectival compounds, coordinated noun-number contexts) that should usually be held for disambiguation; clarified these are candidacy concerns that affect ranking, not hard forbiddances.",
      "Priority Rules: Incorporated those same follow-ups as explicit demotion signals: any candidate that ends immediately before them should be ranked lower (not removed). Also stated that multiword adjectival compounds must stay with their head where possible, and reiterated the minimum-tag fallback for conservative low-confidence placements.",
      "Decision Procedure: Made the two-step flow explicit about demotion (not prohibition): mark generously, then lower rank for candidates that end immediately before listed disambiguating triggers; if minimum boundaries are not met, add conservative low-confidence candidates at safe punctuation/conjunctions and rank them last."
    ],
    "next_changelog": [
      "Role: clarified that marking is generous but the ranking must protect against premature assertions by ensuring the safest candidate(s) are ranked highest; this focuses the model on avoiding placement errors without shrinking candidacy.",
      "Core Principles: added a short, language-neutral positive criterion for high-ranked cuts (prefer cuts that leave a stable predication with core participants) and a reminder to demote (via ranking) candidates that end immediately before tokens likely to continue the phrase — this guides ranking without adding hard forbiddances.",
      "Never Segment: removed the repeated, frozen punctuation/start/end constraints (refer to [Output Rules]) and tightened the forbidden category to only truly impossible splits (non-renderable stretches), avoiding redundant restatement of output-format details.",
      "Examples — Segment: replaced some generic examples with targeted examples illustrating correct segmentation for common placement-failure patterns (agent/voice disambiguation, pronoun/antecedent, multiword adjectival compounds, sentence-boundary segmentation), so the model learns safe placements from examples rather than more prohibitions.",
      "Examples — Do NOT Segment: added realistic Do-NOT lists taken from frequent critic failures (premature cuts before negation, postverbal obliques, relative/complement clauses, short prepositional/temporal fragments, vague pronouns) to show where segmentation should be withheld."
    ]
